WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.652
Second Normal Form becomes especially relevant when a table has a composite key.

00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:10.042
Suppose the row is identified by appointment ID and service code together.

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:13.802
Now ask whether every non-key column depends on that whole combination.

00:00:13.802 --> 00:00:14.812
Appointment start time depends only on the appointment.

00:00:14.812 --> 00:00:19.187
Service name and standard price depend only on the service code.

00:00:19.187 --> 00:00:25.647
Quantity and the captured charge may depend on the combination of appointment and service.

00:00:25.647 --> 00:00:30.272
The columns that depend on only part of the composite key should be moved to the table where that dependency

00:00:30.272 --> 00:00:36.567
belongs. This leads toward separate appointment, service, and appointment-service tables.

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:40.887
The goal is to align each fact with the key that actually determines it.

00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:44.902
Connect this idea to an online store separating customers, orders, products, and order lines.

00:00:44.902 --> 00:00:50.412
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:50.412 --> 00:00:55.172
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:55.172 --> 00:01:00.312
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:05.044
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
